              "vo": "Now jump to payday. Corporate finance uploads an encrypted batch file telling the bank to pay 15 million dong into your account. That file is just a digital note; it carries zero cash inside it. Since both the company's checking account and yours sit inside the same bank, the bank broadcasts nothing across external payment rails. It simply opens one core ledger and changes two lines: 15 million less owed to your company, 15 million more owed to you. Bankers call this an On-Us transfer. Total deposits inside the bank do not move by a single dong. Only the name of the creditor changes. And that text saying your account was credited? That reflects a Credit entry on the bank's ledger — meaning the bank now owes you 15 million.",
