        "srtText": "1\n00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,300\nYour phone buzzes on payday morning: 15 million dong credited to your checking account.\n\n2\n00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:05,850\nYet between bank vaults, not one dong of cash actually moved across town.\n\n3\n00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:13,580\nBecause your employer and you hold accounts at the exact same bank, cash never leaves the building.\n\n4\n00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:20,290\nThe bank simply edits two lines inside one ledger: less owed to your company, more owed to you.\n\n5\n00:00:21,130 --> 00:00:28,140\nUnspent pay sitting idle in checking is called CASA, paying roughly 0.1% yearly interest in this example.\n\n6\n00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:35,090\nAcross 500 coworkers leaving 5 million dong each, the bank builds an instant 2.5 billion dong cash pool.\n\n7\n00:00:35,650 --> 00:00:45,130\nOn that entire 2.5 billion dong pool, the bank pays only 2.5 million dong a year in interest.\n\n8\n00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:54,410\nRaising the same cash with 5% term deposits would cost 125 million dong — fifty times more.\n\n9\n00:00:55,170 --> 00:01:01,450\nIn exchange for steering all those monthly paychecks into one house, your employer often wins lower wire fees or cheaper corporate borrowing rates.\n\n10\n00:01:02,510 --> 00:01:11,730\nSo tonight your salary balance sleeps inside the bank's general ledger — as an obligation the bank owes you while putting your idle cash to work.\n"
